course: world
lesson: 99
title: Transport: from horses to planes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: For most of history people travelled on foot, by boat, or with animals. Boats are among the oldest ways to travel, because water carries heavy loads easily. Horses were tamed about 5,500 years ago, and the wheel was invented at about the same time, so animals could pull carts.
explain: Each new kind of transport solved a problem of the one before. Sailing ships used the free power of the wind but stopped when the wind dropped. The steam engine burned coal to boil water, and the steam pushed the engine; from the 1820s steam trains pulled long lines of wagons on iron rails. Smooth rails cut friction so much that one engine could pull what hundreds of horses could not.
explain: Around 1886 Karl Benz built a car with a petrol engine. In 1908 Henry Ford began making the Model T, and from 1913 his moving assembly line built cars so quickly and cheaply that ordinary families could buy them.
explain: On 17 December 1903 the Wright brothers made the first powered, controlled flight in an aeroplane; it lasted 12 seconds. A plane's wings are shaped and tilted to push air downwards as they move, so the air pushes the plane up. Today jet planes cross an ocean in a few hours — a trip that took weeks by sailing ship.
explain: Faster is not always better. Planes are fast but use a lot of fuel; ships are slow but can carry huge loads cheaply, so most of the world's goods still travel by sea.
fact: Boats are among the oldest kinds of transport.
fact: Horses were tamed about 5,500 years ago, around the time the wheel was invented.
fact: Sailing ships are driven by the wind.
fact: A steam engine burns fuel to boil water, and the steam pushes the engine's parts.
fact: The Stockton and Darlington Railway in England opened in 1825 as the first public railway to use steam engines.
fact: Wheels on smooth iron rails have much less friction than wheels on a rough road.
fact: Karl Benz built one of the first petrol-engined cars around 1886.
fact: Henry Ford's moving assembly line, started in 1913, made cars cheap enough for many families.
fact: The Wright brothers made the first powered, controlled aeroplane flight on 17 December 1903.
fact: A plane's wings push air downwards, and the air pushes the plane upwards; this is called lift.
fact: Jet planes can cross the Atlantic Ocean in about seven hours.
fact: Most of the world's goods are carried by ship, because ships move huge loads cheaply.
exercise: Why were boats one of the first ways to carry heavy loads? | Water holds up heavy loads easily | Boats were faster than anything else | Boats did not need anyone to steer them | Roads were not allowed in those days
exercise: About how long ago were horses tamed? | About 5,500 years ago | About 500 years ago | About 50,000 years ago | About 200 years ago
exercise: What powers a sailing ship? | The wind | Coal | Petrol | Electricity
exercise: How does a steam engine work? | Burning fuel boils water and the steam pushes the engine | The wind blows into the engine and turns it round | Horses inside the engine walk on a wheel | Electricity from the rails turns the wheels
exercise: Why could a steam train pull much more than horses on a road? | The engine was strong and smooth rails cut friction | The wagons on trains weighed nothing at all | Trains always ran downhill to the cities | The rails were giant magnets that pulled the whole train along
exercise: In which country did the first public steam railway open, in 1825? | England | The United States | France | Australia
exercise: Who built one of the first petrol-engined cars around 1886? | Karl Benz | Henry Ford | The Wright brothers | George Stephenson
exercise: Why did Henry Ford's assembly line matter? | It made cars cheap enough for many families | It made the very first car that could also fly | It made cars that needed no fuel | It was the first way to build trains
exercise: When did the Wright brothers make the first powered, controlled flight? | 1903 | 1803 | 1953 | 1925
exercise: How long did the Wright brothers' first flight last? | 12 seconds | 12 minutes | 12 hours | 2 days
exercise: What holds an aeroplane up in the air? | Its wings push air down, so the air pushes it up | It is lighter than the air around it | Its engines point straight down at the ground | Gravity is switched off high above the clouds
exercise: How do most of the world's goods travel between countries? | By ship | By aeroplane | By train | By lorry
exercise: Why are ships used for most goods even though planes are faster? | Ships carry huge loads much more cheaply | Planes are not allowed to carry any goods | Ships are faster than planes over oceans | Goods break if they go up high in the air
exercise: What problem did steam ships solve for sailors? | They could move even when there was no wind | They no longer needed to float on water | They never needed any fuel at all | They could not be damaged or sunk by any storm
test: A farmer must send 20 tonnes of grain to another continent and is in no hurry. Which transport is best? | A ship, because it is cheap for heavy loads | A plane, because it is always the cheapest | A horse and cart across the ocean | A bicycle with a large basket
test: A doctor must get a small box of medicine to another continent by tomorrow. Which transport is best? | A plane | A sailing ship | A canal boat | A steam train
test: Which list puts these in the order they appeared? | Sailing ship, steam train, car, aeroplane | Car, sailing ship, aeroplane, steam train | Aeroplane, car, steam train, sailing ship | Steam train, aeroplane, sailing ship, car
test: A cart on a muddy road and a wagon on iron rails carry the same load. Why does the wagon need far less pulling? | There is much less friction on smooth rails | Rails make the load lighter | Mud pulls the cart towards the ground | Iron rails push the wagon along by magnetism
test: What would happen to a sailing ship on a day with no wind at all? | It would hardly move | It would go faster than usual | It would sink at once | It would move backwards
test: A paper plane stays up longer than a crumpled ball of the same paper. Why? | Its wings push on the air as it moves | The paper becomes lighter when folded | The ball has more gravity than the plane | The plane has a tiny engine inside
test: Which is true? | Cars became common after they were made cheaply on assembly lines | Cars were invented before the wheel | The first flight lasted many hours | Steam engines are powered by the wind
test: Before trains, a trip from London to Edinburgh took days by horse-drawn coach. What did railways change most? | Long journeys became much faster and cheaper | People stopped travelling between cities | Horses were no longer used anywhere at all | The two cities moved closer together
test: Why did early people often build towns beside rivers and coasts? | Boats made travel and trade easier there | Rivers kept away every kind of wild, dangerous animal | Roads could not be built inland | It never rained beside rivers
test: why's most stuff shipped by sea not by plane | Ships move huge loads cheaply | Planes are not allowed to carry goods | Ships are always faster than planes | The sea is shorter than the sky
test: why'd trains go on iron rails, not just roads | Wheels on smooth rails have much less friction | Rails stop the trains from getting lost | Iron rails give the train extra power from the ground | Roads were too narrow for wheels
test: Ava says the Wright brothers first flew in 1950. true? | No, it was on 17 December 1903 | Yes, planes were invented after the war | Yes, but only with a jet engine | No, they flew in 1825
test: my mate reckons Henry Ford invented the car. right? | No, his assembly line made cars cheap; Benz built one of the first cars | Yes, he built the very first car in 1913 | Yes, and also the first aeroplane | No, the Wright brothers invented the car
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a steam engine? | Fuel boils water, and the steam pushes the engine's parts | The wind pushes sails attached to the engine | Petrol explodes inside a cylinder | Electricity from a battery turns a motor
exam: Identify the persons who made the first powered, controlled aeroplane flight, together with the year thereof. | The Wright brothers, in 1903 | Karl Benz, in 1886 | Henry Ford, in 1913 | The Wright brothers, in 1825
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which wheels upon iron rails move more easily than upon a rough road. | Smooth iron rails give much less friction | Iron rails pull the wheels by magnetism | Iron rails reduce the weight of the train | Rough roads have no friction whatsoever
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the carriage of the world's goods. | Most are carried by ship, which moves huge loads cheaply | Most are carried by aeroplane, which is cheapest | Most are carried by horse and cart | Most are carried by bicycle
